nsitivity interval (Brief P6, rev. 11; exploratory-after-results on the existing cells, confirmatory on the shared block).** *Order of events, which the paper tells as it happened:* P5(ii) was predicted to work and failed; the non-identification argument was formulated afterwards as an explanation of the failure; P6 and P7 are the tests of that explanation, not something the pre-registration anticipated. The interval is the honest-CI form of Armstrong–Kolesár 2018/2020 (worst-case bias over a derivative-bounded class, rule-of-thumb M̂) with κ × the in-support magnitude as the relative-magnitudes logic of Rambachan–Roth 2023 (abstract on\n+| C9 assumption-indexed interval (exploratory; block confirmatory) | **P6(1) supported (says little: conservative); P6(2) failed; P6(3) not supported** | B₂ block coverage 0.990, B₁ 0.967 vs HC0 0.70, at 8.4–14.4× and 3.8–5.6× HC0 width (block strata); κ\\* B₂ 0.25 at the threshold; coverage minus width-matched control ≤ 0 for every bound (B₂ vs C2 −0.010 [−0.020, −0.002] block); tails: min 0.30 (B₂), 0.099 (B₁); |bias|/SD > 2: 0.938/0.821 vs HC0 0.153 | a valid conservative device, not informative; the width is not shown to be the price of non-identification (cubic M̂ is noise amplification at narrow f range; exploratory arm with the true M pending); no claim a b\n-**The thesis the results carry (rev. 6).** (1) The *variance* of the regression GATE estimator is predictable from leverage, to a stated departure (d exponent 0.55); (2) the *bias* is predictable from the extrapolation account only with knowledge of the mechanism (exact population prediction, held-out R² 0.99 on log|bias|) and not from any score we computed from the observed data (all below the constant; C6: no evidence of a useful score, intervals wide), and its dependence on degree is mechanism-specific; (3) regression beats DM when the spillover share exceeds roughly the estimator's own noise, the break-even share rising with degree, with the threshold mechanism a sy